TI  - ConfidentNeuro: Conformal Prediction-Based Uncertainty Quantification for Hallucination Risk Scoring in LLM-Assisted Alzheimer's Clinical Decision Support
AB  - Abstract Alzheimer's disease (AD) affects more than 55 million people worldwide. In recent years, clinical decision support (CDS) systems built on large language models (LLMs) have become increasingly common in neurological care. These models hallucinate, generating clinically plausible but factually incorrect responses. The existing evaluation methods measure overall accuracy, leaving clinicians without per-response uncertainty or clear distrust signals. We present ConfidentNeuro, a conformal prediction framework for assessing the risk of hallucination in LLM-assisted AD decision support. We also introduce ConfidentNeuro-Bench, which includes 150 clinical question-answer pairs across five AD categories. These were gathered using PubMed MeSH queries, and each was linked to its source PMID. GPT-4o, Claude Haiku, and Mixtral-8x7B were each tested zero-shot, using conformal prediction with semantic similarity as the nonconformity score. GPT-4o and Claude Haiku showed empirical coverage of 97.1%. Whereas Mixtral-8x7B reached 95.7%, both above the 90% target. The calibration audit indicated that GPT-4o is miscalibrated (ECE = 0.213). As a result, token-level log probabilities are an unreliable confidence signal in clinical use. Under perturbation audit across eight input modifications, confidence barely changed, staying around 0.56–0.62, despite a twofold variation in semantic disruption. This raises a key issue of a systemic failure in self-assessment. We ran ablation experiments to examine each component’s contribution. ConfidentNeuro assigns a risk label (LOW/MEDIUM/HIGH) to each response and makes it visible to the clinician without requiring access to the model internals or retraining. This results in uncertainty estimates that are consistent across different models, making them easier to use in neurological care.
