TI  - TurkCuisineBench: A source-grounded short-answer benchmark for large language models’ factual knowledge of Turkish cuisine and culinary heritage
AB  - Abstract Culturally grounded language-model evaluation remains limited for specialized Turkish knowledge, and exact-string scoring can miss semantically correct short answers. This study introduces TurkCuisineBench, a 72-item Turkish short-answer benchmark for factual knowledge of cuisine and culinary heritage. Items were derived from 38 accessible official or institutional source URLs, balanced across six knowledge domains, independently validated, and frozen before model execution. Source grounding constrains item construction and human adjudication rather than supplying evidence to models at inference time: eight proprietary and open-weight endpoints answered every item under a common closed-book, stateless, answer-only protocol. Of 576 expected responses, 574 were technically valid. Blinded human review resolved all 326 valid non-exact responses; a prespecified 82-response overlap achieved 97.6% pre-adjudication decision agreement (Cohen’s κ = .875). Semantic accuracy differed substantially across endpoints (13.9%–70.8%; Holm-adjusted p < .001). Overall semantic correctness was 230/574 (40.1%), compared with 187/574 (32.6%) under normalized exact match, recovering 43 correct responses (+ 7.49 percentage points; item-cluster bootstrap 95% confidence interval [4.36, 11.19]). The prespecified contrast between items without lexical answer cues and those with limited cues was not supported. TurkCuisineBench demonstrates a compact, auditable approach to culturally specific factual evaluation while explicitly limiting claims to the sampled institutional-source record rather than treating it as exhaustive culinary knowledge.
