TI  - Evaluation Dimension Determines Conclusion: A Systematic Study of SFT and CPT Effects on Institutional Financial Knowledge Injection
AB  - Companion work (DKSE-FinBench) diagnosed that institutional-private knowledge (L4/L5) is a universal blind spot for LLMs and hypothesized a remedy mapping: SFT for alignment, CPT for knowledge injection. We systematically validate these hypotheses through 8 training experiments (3 SFT versions and 5 CPT versions) on Qwen3-8B, evaluated on 290 probes across three dimensions: (i) 200 mechanical probes, (ii) 40 natural business questions, and (iii) 40 general-finance probes (L1–L3 catastrophic-forgetting check). Three findings emerge: (1) SFT universally fails—all three versions score 4 to 18 percent, far below the 38 percent baseline; (2) CPT's effectiveness depends entirely on the evaluation dimension: on mechanical probes five CPT variants tie the baseline (within plus-or-minus 3.5 pp noise), yet on natural business questions CPT yields +20 pp (flow-understanding +50 pp); (3) sample size determines conclusions: 50 probes falsely suggested CPT surpasses baseline by +2 pp, but 200 probes revealed this was noise. These yield a treatment division: SFT for alignment, CPT for concept and flow, RAG for precise values. The evaluation-dimension finding warns that single-dimensional benchmarks risk false positives or negatives about training-method effectiveness.
